  "text": "Here's the complete implementation of the `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function that meets the specified requirements, along with the unit tests:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### How it Works:\n\n- The function starts by checking if the input list is empty \u2014 if it is, it returns an empty list.\n- It initializes the `result` list with the first element of the input list.\n- Then, it iterates through the rest of the list and only appends an element to `result` if it's different from the last element in `result`.\n- This effectively removes **consecutive duplicates**, leaving only the first occurrence of each set of duplicates.\n\n### Example:\n\n```python\nremove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4])\n# Output: [1, 2, 3, 4]\n```\n\nThis solution is efficient and handles all the test cases correctly.",
